Kirjath-arba.; Kiriath-Arba.

@AC 2909. "In Kirjath-arba, this is Hebron in the Land of Canaan" (Genesis 23:2) = in the Church; (for) "Kirjath-arba" = the Church as to truth.

@--[2]. As to Kirjath-arba which is Hebron, it was the region where dwelt Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. Illustrated. From the representation of these three... it is evident that Kirjath-arba which is Hebron represented the Church before Jerusalem did so.

@--[3]. That in course of time every Church decreases, until nothing of faith and charity remains, and that it is then destroyed, was represented by Kirjath-arba which is Hebron, in that it was possessed by the Anakim. Illustrated.

@2981. It is there called "Kirjath-arba this is Hebron," because by "Kirjath-arba" is signified the Church as to truth, and by "Hebron," the Church as to good. Here, however, it is no longer called Kirjath-arba, but "Hebron," because it treats of one who is regenerate, who no longer acts from truth but from good.

@4613. "Mamre Kirjath-arba" (Genesis 35:27) = the state of the Natural as to truth... "Kirjath-arba" = the Church as to truth, thus truth.

Kiss.; Osculari.
Kissing.; Osculatio.

@AC 3573. "Kiss me my son" (Genesis 27:26) = whether he could be united. To "kiss" = unition and conjunction from affection. Kissing, which is an external thing, is nothing else than the affection of conjunction, which is an internal one: they also correspond.

@3574. "He approached and kissed him" (Genesis 27:27) = presence and unition... To "kiss" = unition or conjunction from affection. Illustrated.

@--. To "kiss the Son" (Psalms 2:12) = to be conjoined with the Lord by the faith of love.

@--. "Let justice and peace kiss" (Psalms 85:10) = let them conjoin themselves.

@--[2]. They are said to "kiss the calves" (Hosea 13:2), that is, embrace magic, and adjoin themselves to it.

@--[end]. "Every mouth which hath not kissed Baal" (1 Kings 19:18). To "kiss" = to conjoin themselves from affection, thus to worship.

@3800. "Jacob kissed Rachel" (Genesis 29:11) = love towards interior truths. To "kiss" = unition and conjunction from affection; consequently love...

@3808. "And kissed him" (Genesis 29:13) = initiation. To "kiss" = conjunction from affection; here, initiation to this conjunction; for initiation is what precedes conjunction.

@4139. "And hast not permitted me to kiss my sons and my daughters" (Genesis 31:28) = disjunction... as to truths and goods.

@4215. "(Laban) kissed his sons and his daughters" (Genesis 31:55) = the acknowledgement of these Truths, and of the affections of the same. To "kiss" = conjunction from affection, consequently acknowledgement; for where there is conjunction by good and truth, there is the acknowledgement of them.

@--[2]. Conjunction from affection produces kissing (by the correspondence)...

@4353. "He kissed him" (Genesis 33:4) = interior conjunction from love. To "kiss" = conjunction from love; here, interior conjunction.

@5312. "Upon thy mouth all my people shall kiss" (Genesis 41:40) = that everything there shall be under obedience to him. To "kiss upon his mouth" = to acknowledge and do what he commands, thus to obey.

@5929. "He kissed all his brethren" (Genesis 45:15) = adjunction from grace. To "kiss" = conjunction from affection; here, adjunction from grace. Explained.

@6260. "He kissed them" (Genesis 48:10) = conjunction from the affection of truth... because there follows "he embraced them," by which is signified conjunction from the affection of good; for embracing is a more interior and thence closer affection than kissing, as is the affection of good than the affection of truth.

@6501. "He kissed him" (Genesis 50:1) = the first conjunction. To "kiss" = conjunction from affection; here, the first conjunction, because in what follows it treats of a closer conjunction.

@7057. "And kissed him" (Exodus 4:27) = the affection of conjunction. To "kiss" = conjunction from affection. @8664.

@AR 245[2]. "The wings kissed one another" (Ezekiel 1:23) = to act conjointly and unanimously.

@875. The two Angels embraced and kissed each other. @TCR 386.

@CL 20[2]. He then kissed her, and said. Now thou art mine...

@208[end]. From the mouths of their wives which they kiss.

@TCR 37[2]. They kiss nature as the creatress of the universe. @508[3].

@45[2]. Robbers, who kiss each other when they are at robberies...

@80. The satan kissed her, and said. Oh my Adonis!

@418[end]. Then the goods kiss each other and conjoin themselves.

@509[end]. He kisses sin daily, like...

@SE 2973. The black cow kissed her...

@4494[2]. She kissed him, and then he was tortured...

@4745. See <Charles XII>.

@AE 283[13]. "The wings kissed one another" = the consociation and conjunction of all things there by the Lord.

@587[11]. To "kiss the calves" = to become merely natural.

@684[13]. "Kiss ye the Son" = conjunction with the Lord by love. To "kiss" = conjunction from love. @850[3].

@DeConj 108. See <Embrace>.

